# Activity regulation and applications of metal–organic framework-based nanozymes

## abstract
As a new generation of artificial enzymes, nanozymes show outstanding advantages such as high stability, low cost, and facile synthesis, which endow them with promising applications in biomedical and environmental fields. Among the various reported nanozymes, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) could mimic the active center of natural enzymes and provide a hydrophobic environment, which makes MOFs attractive alternatives to natural enzymes. Owing to the highly structural diversity and tailorability of MOFs, rational design will contribute to improve the activity of MOF-based nanozymes and promote their potential applications in both biomedical and environmental fields. Therefore, a comprehensive summary of activity regulatory strategies of MOF-based nanozymes is urgently needed. Firstly, we summarized the activity regulatory strategies of MOFs with intrinsic enzyme-like activities via modulation of metal nodes, ligands, structures and morphologies. Then the applications of MOF-based nanozymes in biosensing, hazardous degradation, antibacterial, and cancer therapy were also introduced. Finally, the current challenges and future perspectives were discussed in depth. It is highly expected that this review will provide a better understanding on the rational design of novel high-performance MOF-based nanozymes.

Abstract As a new generation of artificial enzymes, nanozymes show outstanding advantages such as high stability, low cost, and facile synthesis, which endow them with promising applications in biomedical and environmental fields. Among the various reported nanozymes, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) could mimic the active center of natural enzymes and provide a hydrophobic environment, which makes MOFs attractive alternatives to natural enzymes. Owing to the highly structural diversity and tailorability of MOFs, rational design will contribute to improve the activity of MOF-based nanozymes and promote their potential applications in both biomedical and environmental fields. Therefore, a comprehensive summary of activity regulatory strategies of MOF-based nanozymes is urgently needed. Firstly, we summarized the activity regulatory strategies of MOFs with intrinsic enzyme-like activities via modulation of metal nodes, ligands, structures and morphologies. Then the applications of MOF-based nanozymes in biosensing, hazardous degradation, antibacterial, and cancer therapy were also introduced. Finally, the current challenges and future perspectives were discussed in depth. It is highly expected that this review will provide a better understanding on the rational design of novel highperformance MOF-based nanozymes.
Keywords Metal–organic frameworks (MOF)-based nanozymes; Activity regulation; Biosensing; Cancer therapy; Hazardous degradation; Antibacterial
## 1 Introduction
As powerful biological catalysts, natural enzymes play significant roles in life-related processes, which can speed up various biocatalytic reactions via catalyzing the conversion of biomolecules under mild conditions [1]. Except for biocatalytic reactions in life processes, natural enzymes are widely applied in industrial, medical, and many other fields [2]. However, natural enzymes suffer inherent drawbacks, including high manufacturing and purification cost, sensitive to temperature and $\mathrm{\DeltapH}$ , which seriously hinder their practical applications [3]. Over the past decades, nanomaterials with enzyme-like characteristics termed as ‘‘nanozymes’’ gradually become potential alternatives for natural enzymes due to their attractive traits, such as facile synthesis, high stability, and durability toward harsh environments [4–6]. Since Yang et al. discovered $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles with intrinsic peroxidase (POD)-like activity in 2007 [7], a variety of nanomaterials have been reported to exhibit various kinds of enzyme-like activities [8–10], including noble metals (Au [11], Pd [12], Pt [13], Ag [14], etc.), transition metal oxides $\mathrm{(Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [15], $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ [16], $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ [17], etc.), and carbon nanomaterials (graphene oxide [18], carbon nanotubes [19], etc.). However, these inorganic materials suffer from low specific surface area and limited structural flexibility.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are distinct crystalline porous materials composed of metal ions or clusters coordinately bridged by organic ligands [20–23]. Since it was firstly reported in 1995, research on new MOFs topologies, modified MOFs structures, MOFs composites, and MOFs derivatives has attracted considerable interest and achieved rapid development over the past 20 years [24, 25]. Owing to their highly structural diversity and tailorability, diverse MOFs were demonstrated to exhibit excellent physicochemical properties and were widely used in various fields, such as gas adsorption and separation, catalysis, proton conduction, drug delivery, and antibacterial [26–31]. Recently, owing to the structural similarity with natural enzymes, a number of MOFs have been shown to be able to mimic natural enzymes, making them to be research hotspots in the fields of nanozymes.
Compared to noble metal and oxide-based nanozymes, MOF-based nanozymes show specific advantages: firstly, the open pores and interconnected channels in the MOF network structure make metal active sites accessible, resulting in the enhanced enzyme-like activities. Secondly, considering the regular arrangement of metal nodes and ligands in MOFs, active sites dispersed uniformly in the periodic framework without aggregation, which hold huge potential to achieve novel nanozymes with high catalytic activity and stability. Thirdly, high tailorability of metal nodes, ligands as well as pore size give prospects to the rational design of various kinds of MOF-based nanozymes [32–35]. To date, MOFs with different metal nodes have been reported to exhibit intrinsic oxidoreductase (POD, oxidase, and superoxide dismutase) or hydrolase (DNase and phosphatase)-like activities, such as $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOF (UiO-66, PCN-222, PCN-224, and NU-1000), Fe-MOF (MIL-100, MIL-53, and MIL-88B), and $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOF (HKUST-1 and CuBTC) [36, 37]. On account of their highly structural diversity and tailorability, MOF-based nanozymes have become potential candidates in nanozymes and been widely applied in biological medicine and environmental treatment. Even so, the activity and selectivity of MOFsbased biomimetic catalysts are still unsatisfying compared to natural enzymes and there’s still much room for improvement.
Recently, reviews on MOF-based nanozyme have been reported with different focuses, including synthesis methods, types of enzyme mimics, influence factors on catalytic activity, together with various applications [30, 36, 38]. However, to the best of our knowledge, activity regulatory strategies based on the topological structures of MOFbased nanozymes are rarely overviewed. Taking into account of the abundance of active sites and the potential for structural regulation, this review mainly focused on the regulation strategies of MOF-based nanozymes as well as the potential applications. Therefore, the activity regulatory strategies via modulation of metal nodes, ligands, structures and morphologies were firstly summarized and discussed. Subsequently, the potential applications of MOFbased nanozymes were also presented, including biosensing, hazardous degradation, antibacterial, and cancer therapy. Finally, the major challenges and opportunities for MOF-based nanozymes were briefly discussed. We believe that this progress report will provide new insights into the design and regulation of novel MOF-based nanozymes.
## 2 Activity regulatory strategies of MOF-based nanozymes
Although various MOFs have been reported to mimic natural enzymes, their catalytic activity and selectivity remains unsatisfied. To enhance their catalytic activity, various efficient approaches have been developed. In this section, we will summarize the major activity regulatory strategies of MOF-based nanozymes based on their topological structure, including modulation of metal node, ligand, and structure. The detailed information of these regulation strategies is summarized in Table 1 for reference.
## 2.1 Tuning metal nodes
For most reported MOF-based nanozymes, the catalytic activities originate from the unsaturated metal centers among all the metal nodes of MOF frameworks. Thus, tuning the metal nodes of MOFs is supposed to be a feasible strategy to modulate the catalytic activity, such as tuning the metal valence, heteroatom substituting, and functional group modification.
## 2.1.1 Tuning metal valence of nodes
The chemical valence states of the metal redox couple and their ratio to some extant determine the catalytic activity of the MOF-based nanozymes [39–44]. Ye and coworkers prepared Ce-MOF with intrinsic oxidase (OXD)-like activity, while the activity was very low since there was only $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ in the as-prepared Ce-MOF that lacked the capacity for catalytic oxidation [39]. To improve the OXD-like activity of Ce-MOF, they introduced $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ via in situ partial oxidation synthetic strategy. Owing to the transformation between $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ , the mixed valence state Ce-MOF showed excellent catalytic activity, in which the $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ ions quickly oxidized $_{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) substrate to form blue oxidized TMB (ox-TMB). In this process, Ce4? r educed to Ce3? and then ‘‘spontaneously’’ recycled back to $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ . Based on the OXD-like activity, Ce-MOF was successfully applied in high-throughput colorimetric detection of biothiols in serum samples (Fig. 1a). Gu and coworkers also found that the abundant $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}/\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$ couple sites in ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}66({\mathrm{Ce}})$ endowed them with the ability to selectively catalyze the hydrolysis of high-energy phosphate bonds (HEPBs) of adenosine triphosphate (ATP) and adenosine diphosphate (ADP) under physiological conditions [42].
## 2.1.2 Heteroatom substituting
Substituting metal atoms in node sites of MOFs by heteroatoms with similar atomic radius shows obvious influence on the catalytic performance of MOF-based nanozymes [45–49]. Zn-MOF (MFU-4l) was reported to catalytically degrade organophosphorus agent due to the presence of $Z_{\mathrm{{n}(I I)-O H}}$ groups on the metal nodes resembled the active sites in carbonic anhydrase. Heteroatom (Cu, Co and Ni) was introduced into the $Z\mathrm{n}_{5}$ nodes to study how the metal nodes influence catalytic activity of MFU-4l. The results showed that Cu-MFU-4l exhibited excellent catalytic performance for the hydrolysis of nerve agent simulant dimethyl (4-nitrophenyl) phosphate (DMNP) under basic aqueous conditions due to the strong Lewis acidic active sites of $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}\mathrm{{-OH}}}$ (Fig. 1b). Nevertheless, hydrolytic activities of Co-MFU-4l and Ni-MFU-4l were much lower under same conditions [48].
## 2.1.3 Functional group modification of metal nodes
Coordinatively unsaturated metal sites (CUS) in MOFs usually act as active Lewis acid sites and can be modified by organic functional groups to modulate the chemical environment of metal nodes [50, 51]. Hwang and coworkers functionalized MIL-100(Fe) (where MIL represents materials from the Lavoisier Institute) by grafting aliphatic diamines with different carbon chains between the two nitrogen atoms on its CUS to regulate the charge density and hydrophobicity of Fe active sites (Fig. 1c, d) [51]. After amine functionalization, the numbers of negative charges on the particles increased which could help to improve their affinity toward positively charged TMB molecules through an electrostatic attraction. Among diverse amine-functionalized MOFs, N, N, N’, N’-tetramethyl-1,4-butanediamine (TMBDA)-functionalized MIL100(Fe) (TMBDA-MIL-100(Fe)) exhibited the highest POD activity due to the synergetic effect of the enhanced negative potential and optimum hydrophobicity.
## 2.2 Tuning ligands
As another important component of MOFs materials, organic linkers have important effects on the topology, properties, as well as catalytic performance of MOFs. For example, molecular size of ligand can determine the pore size and channel shape of MOFs, thereby affecting the exposure and accessibility of active sites [52–55]. Besides, organic linkers are highly modifiable, which can be functionalized by various organic groups and influence the physicochemical properties of the overall MOFs structures.
## 2.2.1 Introducing active metallic ligands
The metal nodes are generally used as the active sites, but ligands with metals could also be introduced into MOFs to achieve catalytic activities [56–59]. For example, PCN-222 composed of $Z\mathrm{r}$ metal node and porphyrin ligand [60], shows negligible POD-like activity. While the porphyrin center with four $\mathbf{N}$ atoms facilitates transition metals chelation, such as Fe to construct Fe-porphyrin which is similar to the active center in natural POD to introduce enzyme-like activity. As early as in 2012, Zhou and coworkers firstly reported that after chelating Fe in porphyrin center, PCN-222(Fe) showed excellent POD-like catalytic activity due to the analogous active center in PCN-222(Fe) and natural POD, such as horseradish peroxides (HRP) and heme [54]. Recently, Wang and coworkers discovered that after chelating Mn in porphyrin ligand, the as-prepared PCN-224(Mn) exhibited remarkable OXD-like ability to trigger the TMB color reaction, while the original $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF (PCN-224) showed very weak activity [58]. Besides, MOF-818 [37] and MOF-919 [61] with ligands containing trinuclear copper centers could also act as active sites to perform OXD-like activity (Fig. 2a) [37], especially for MOF-818 which showed efficient catechol OXD activity with good specificity and no POD-like characteristics rather than $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ and $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ NPs.
## 2.2.2 Functional group modification of ligands
Although the active sites in MOF-based nanozymes are usually located on metal nodes, functional group modification of MOF ligands can greatly affect the chemical environment around metal nodes, thus influencing catalytic activity [62–64]. In addition, adsorption of substrates can be effectively promoted through electrostatic adsorption and hydrogen bonding between the modified functional groups and substrate molecule [65, 66]. Furthermore, the organic linkers can be flexibly modified by various functional groups, which provides the possibility for the rational design of MOF-based nanozymes. Wei and coworkers employed a set of substituted MIL-53(Fe) with similar coordination to investigate the relationship between structure and OXD-mimicking activity [67]. The results revealed a Hammett-type structure–activity linear free energy relationship of the as-prepared MOFs catalysts, in which increasing the Hammett $\sigma_{\mathrm{m}}$ value with electron withdrawing ligands $(-\mathrm{NO}_{2})$ enhances the OXD-mimicking activity of substituted MIL-53(Fe) (Fig. 2b).
## 2.3 Tuning structure/morphology
## 2.3.1 Amorphous MOF
Despite from conventional crystalline MOFs, amorphous MOFs are non-crystalline metal organic networks display short-range order rather than long-range periodic order within the structure [68–72]. Recently, a small but growing number of amorphous MOFs are synthesized and exhibit unique properties of the amorphous domain such as isotropy, absence of grain boundaries, abundant defects and active sites, and flexibility. Moreover, they also possess the properties of crystalline MOFs including relatively high stabilities and ultrahigh surface areas. Huang and coworkers synthesized amorphous Cu-MOF (CA-Cu) via the facile solvothermal reaction of cyanuric acid and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ to mimic the N-Cu coordinated environment from active sites in natural laccase and catecholase [68]. Owing to high catalytic activity, excellent stability, and good substrate universality, CA-Cu nanozyme exhibited broad application prospects in the degradation of chlorophenol and diphenol as well as the detection of dopamine. Another amorphous
Cu-MOF based on guanosine monophosphate (GMP) coordinated copper was also discovered to catalytically oxidize a diverse range of phenol containing substrates such as hydroquinone, naphthol, catechol, and epinephrine that demonstrated excellent laccase-like activity (Fig. 3a) [69].
## 2.3.2 2D MOF
Compared to the conventional 3D bulk MOF nanozymes, 2D MOF nanosheets possess larger surface area and more accessible active sites for the substrate molecules with a smaller diffusion barrier [57, 73–76]. At present, a variety of 2D MOFs with enzyme-like activities have been reported, including M-TCPP $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}$ , Co, Zn, Zr; TCPP represents tetra(4-carboxyphenyl) porphine), $\mathrm{Cu(HBTC)}{\cdot}1$ , Ni-MOF nanosheet, and $\mathrm{Cu}$ -HHTP. In particular, M-TCPP containing iron porphyrin ligand has been found to be the most active POD-like nanozyme due to the similar hemelike active sites (Fig. 3b) [74].
## 2.3.3 Other regulation strategies
Other activity regulation strategies were also reported to improve the enzyme-like properties of MOFs [77–79]. Lin and coworkers investigated the effects of structural defects of MOFs consisted of cobalt and 2-methylimidazole ligand on the OXD-like activity [80]. With an increased defect degree, OXD-like activity toward different substrates were enhanced by 2-5 times. Structural defects induced by sulfur doping may destroy the equilibrium state between Co and nitrogen in the ligand and distort the crystal lattice and results in enhancing the adsorption of oxygen and thus promoting the OXD-like activity. In another example, Farha and coworkers investigated how the connected number of ligands around metal nodes affected the hydrolysis efficiency of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs [81]. MOF-808 featuring the lowest connected number (6-connected) displayed the highest hydrolysis rates compared to NU-1000 (molecular formula $\mathrm{Zr_{6}(\mu_{3}{-}O H)_{8}(-O H)_{8}(T B A P y)_{2})}$ and UiO-66 (where UiO represents University of Oslo) with the connected numbers of 8-connected and 12-connected, respectively.
## 3 Applications of MOF-based nanozymes
With the attractive features of MOF nanozymes, they have been found to hold great potential for wide applications in the biological and environmental field. Generally, these applications can be classified into the following four categories (Table 2): (1) biosensing; (2) treating bacterial infection; (3) cancer therapy; and (4) degradation of chemical warfare agent.
## 3.1 Biosensing
The sensing of biomolecules (both small and macromolecules) and ions is another important application of MOF-based nanozymes owing to their intrinsic enzymelike activities [31, 75, 82, 83]. MOFs with intrinsic PODlike activities have been widely used to detect $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ by employing TMB as a substrate and monitoring the absorbance changes at $652~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . When combined with glucose OXD (GOx), MOFs could detect glucose via GOx-POD cascade reaction. For example, $\mathrm{\DeltaXu}$ and coworkers [84] reported that the method based on the GOx-POD cascade reaction could detect glucose concentrations in a wide range $(1{-}500~\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ and as low as $0.487~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . The $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generated from the glucose OXD catalyzed oxidation of glucose with $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ was immediately oxidized in situ by the Fe-MOF, thus decreasing the diffusion resistance and further accelerating the decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Fig. 4). As a result, as compared to the free enzyme system, the integrated enzyme system for the detection of glucose demonstrated remarkably great reusability as well as strong temperature adaptation and acid–base tolerance. Other small biomolecules such as uric acid, dopamine, ascorbic acid, and thiamine can also be detected directly or indirectly using various MOF-based nanozymes [85–88]. For example, Badoei-Dalfard et al. [89] reported that using a thorium-based MOF (Th-MOF) mimicking uricase could determine the uric acid concentration through monitoring the generated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ by the oxidation of uric acid.
Apart from small biomolecules, MOF-based nanozymes can also be used to detect metal ions. Ce-MOFs were reported to show OXD-like activity due to the mixed valence of Ce ions. When conjugating with single-stranded DNA (ssDNA), catalytic activity of Ce-MOF was inhibited because of the block of active Ce sites. While $\mathrm{Hg^{+}}$ can react with the conjugated ssDNA and re-expose Ce active sites. Inspired by these features, Tan and coworkers developed a colorimetric assay for $\mathrm{Hg}$ detection which could detect $\mathrm{Hg(II)}$ with high selectivity over other metal ions and the minimum detection value was as low as $10.5~\mathrm{nmol{\cdot}L^{-1}}$ [90].
## 3.2 Treating bacterial infection
Bacterial infection has become a global public health challenge [91]. The great demand for fighting with multiple drug-resistant bacteria calls for exploring new cost-effective and promising antibacterial nanoantibiotic [92]. Recently, MOF-based nanozymes have been discovered for efficient infection treatment, benefiting from their specific structures, compositions and coordination environment [36]. In general, the POD-mimicking MOFs are mostly applied for treating bacteria, which can catalyze the decomposition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to generate highly toxic OH. For example, Qu and coworkers synthesized a 2D MOF(Cu$\mathrm{TCPP(Fe)},$ ) with GOx [93], which could mimic POD-like activity and realize the self-activated cascade system. Owing to the decreased $\mathrm{\pH}$ along with the generation of gluconic acid and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ by GOx, the POD-like activity of
Cu-TCPP(Fe) could be dramatically enhanced in the acid environment and then more extremely toxic OH was efficiently generated, resulting in effectively improved antibacterial performance (Fig. 5a) [93]. In another example, Zhuang’s group [94] fabricated an OXD-mimic CoMOF, which could rapidly catalyze the oxidation of lipids, resulting in bacterial cell membrane rupture and inactivation. Specially, the Co-MOF exhibited outstanding durability of up to four weeks and was fully recyclable. Surprisingly, the bactericidal effect was achieved within $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ .
Except for POD or OXD, the hydrolase-mimicking MOFs are also applied for eliminating the biofilm, always combining with POD or OXD. For example, Qu’s group fabricated a MOF/Au-Ce-based nanozyme with POD-like and DNase-like activities, which could hydrolyze eDNA and disperse biofilms based on DNase-like activity and then kill exposed bacteria through acting as POD mimic, for avoiding persistent infection (Fig. 5b) [95].
## 3.3 Cancer therapy
On account of the characteristics of tumor microenvironment (TME) with overexpressed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and weak acidic $\mathrm{\pH}$ , MOF-based nanozymes possessed intrinsic catalase-like activities can effectively convert the overexpressed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ for alleviating tumor hypoxia [96, 97]. Lin and coworkers reported a Fe-MOF built from iron-oxo clusters and porphyrin ligands, Fe-TBP, which could decompose intracellular $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to produce $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and overcome tumor hypoxia [98]. The generated $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ subsequently converted to cytotoxic singlet oxygen $(^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ by photoexcited porphyrins, sensitizing photodynamic therapy, and priming non-inflamed tumors for cancer immunotherapy. In addition, POD-mimic MOFs can catalyze the endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into highly toxic OH under mild acidic TME triggered by Fenton or Fenton-like reactions [99–104]. Sun and coworkers developed 2D MOF $\mathrm{Pt/Cu-TCPP(Fe)}$ nanosheets loaded with cisplatin, which could generate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ [105]. Then the released ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ion from MOF could further convert the generated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to produce abundant ROS through Fenton-like reaction in cancer cells, which gave rise to oxidative damage in the surrounding protein, DNA, and lipid and thus the sensitivity to cisplatin, further effectively enhancing the anticancer efficiency (Fig. 6a).
Except for chemodynamic therapy, numerous researchers have investigated the use of MOF-based nanozymes for photodynamic (PDT), photothermal therapy, and immunotherapy. For example, a nanoscale MOF (Fe-TBP) reported by Lan et al. [98] could function as a sensitizer to generate ${}^{1}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ by PDT in hypoxic conditions. The tumor cells consequently produced tumor-associated antigens, marking them for destruction by the immune system. Thus, this system served as a ‘‘tumor vaccine’’ and induced a systemic antitumor response. Surprisingly, the results showed that a $90\%$ tumor cure rate could be achieved by applying the MOF in the mice by PDT (Fig. 6b) [98].
## 3.4 Degradation of chemical warfare agent
Nerve agents containing phosphate ester bonds, as a typical class of highly toxic chemical warfare agents (CWA) [106, 107], could be hydrolyzed by phosphotriesterase (PTE). The active center in PTE features two zinc(II) metal ions bridged by hydroxyl ligand. One zinc(II) ion activates the phosphate ester and the other one transfers the OH to cleave the -OR group of the substrate [108]. Similar to natural PTE, Zr-based MOFs contain two $Z\mathrm{r}$ metal ions bridged by hydroxyl ligand were widely reported to display hydrolysis activity toward nerve agent simulants, including UiO-66, UiO-67, NU-1000, MOF-808, PCN-222/MOF-545 and their analogues [106, 109, 110].
Except for nerve agent simulants, other highly toxic chemical warfare agent, such as GD (soman) and VX $(\mathbf{C}_{11}\mathbf{H}_{26}\mathbf{NO}_{2}\mathbf{PS}^{\mathrm{~}}$ ) were also demonstrated to be degraded by these $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOFs in several minutes (Fig. 7) [109]. For example, NU-1000 is extraordinarily effective for the hydrolysis of GD with $t_{1/2}$ (react time with $50\%$ conversion) as low as $3~\mathrm{min}$ . Moreover, these hydrolysis catalysts can be integrated onto fabrics or fibers substrates and be used in masks or protective clothing [111]. For example,
Farha and coworkers integrated MOFs and polymeric bases (linear polyethyleneimine) onto textile fibers for the hydrolysis of nerve agent [112]. The as-prepared textile materials not only showed high hydrolytic activity toward DMNP and GD, but also exhibited high robustness under real-life atmospheres and conditions.
## 4 Summary and outlook
In this paper, we have summarized the activity regulatory strategies of nanozymes, including modulation of metal nodes, ligands, structure and morphology, and shown that MOF-based nanozymes have great potential for various applications ranging from biomedicine to environmental treatment. Although great efforts have been devoted, the investigation of MOF-based nanozymes is still in the infant stage compared to the more rapid development of MOFs in other fields. There are still some practical issues needed to be carefully considered in future investigations:
(1) Activity. Compared with natural enzymes and most metal-based nanozymes, the catalytic activity of the MOF-based nanozymes has much room for enhancement. Therefore, it is urgently needed to develop novel MOF-based nanozymes with high catalytic activity. The fabrication of new structure (e.g., MOF with more surface area and more accessible active sites), or introducing the activity unit of natural enzymes through specific design and the exploration of new modulation strategies (e. g., tuning the coordination environment of active sites), may help to design highly active MOF-based nanozymes.
(2) Specificity. MOF-based nanozyme also has the bottleneck problem of lack of selectivity, which seriously hinders their applications, especially for biosensors. Therefore, more attention should be paid to designing nanozymes mimicking natural enzymes not only in catalytic function but also in spatial structure. In view of the unique three-dimensional structure and the great potential for structure modulation, it is promising to construct the specific substrate binding pocket for improving the catalytic specificity of MOF-based nanozymes. The use of molecular imprinting polymer is a feasible route to realize the selectively catalytic reaction.
(3) Stability. Another challenge for the practical applications of MOF-based nanozymes is instability under various conditions (e.g., solvents, pH, water), especially for a prolonged period of time. Therefore, more efficient approaches of properly improving the poor stability of MOF-based nanozymes are required. To choose proper synthesis method including de novo synthesis and/or post-synthetic structural processing can be a practical approach for developing highly stable MOF in diverse catalytic reactions, which will benefit both their applications and recyclability.
Taken together, due to their enzyme-like feature and unique structural characteristics, MOF-based nanozymes will draw increasing amounts of interest from the scientific community. It is anticipated that more novel MOFs will be designed that stimulate natural enzymes both in terms of function and spatial structure. Additionally, the poor activity, selectivity, and instability issues with MOF-based nanozymes will be addressed with advanced synthetic technology. In the near future, novel MOF-based nanozyme will appear, which is comparable to or even surpassing that of natural enzymes. These developments will significantly expand their potential applications.
